{"slides": [
 {"id": "ps1_slide", "layout": "BLANK", "index": 28, "elements": [
  {"id": "ps1_title", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.3, "w": 9, "h": 0.6, "text": "Five Psychological Experiments", "size": 24, "bold": true, "font": "Merriweather"},
  {"id": "ps1_take", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.95, "w": 9, "h": 0.7, "text": "One configuration for all five studies. The chunks reproduce the effects of familiarity; the misses need a limit on memory or categories formed from few instances.", "size": 14, "bold": true, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ps1_tbl", "type": "table", "x": 0.5, "y": 1.75, "w": 9, "size": 14, "font": "Lato", "row_h": 0.42,
   "col_widths": [2.3, 5.1, 1.6], "header": true, "col_align": ["START", "START", "START"],
   "rows": [["Study", "TRELLIS against people", "Reproduced"],
            ["Chess recall by skill", "7.2, 9.7, 12.9 pieces; people 4, 8, 16", "Ordering yes, values partly"],
            ["Adult segmentation", "r = .93 length, .90 exposure; word types backward", "Two of three"],
            ["Infant segmentation", "Words more familiar in 16 of 16 infants", "Direction yes"],
            ["Skilled digit memory", "Span 16.6 at session 865; DD 104", "Partly"],
            ["Artificial grammar", "Interaction 23.6 (24.7); grammaticality 1.4 (22.0)", "No"]]},
  {"id": "ps1_foot", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 5.0, "w": 8.4, "h": 0.4, "text": "Chase & Simon (1973); Frank et al. (2010); Saffran et al. (1996); Richman et al. (1995); Knowlton & Squire (1996).", "size": 11, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"},
  {"id": "ps1_num", "type": "text", "x": 9, "y": 5.1, "w": 0.5, "h": 0.4, "text": "30", "size": 12, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"}]},
 {"id": "ps2_slide", "layout": "BLANK", "index": 29, "elements": [
  {"id": "ps2_title", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.3, "w": 9, "h": 0.6, "text": "Word Segmentation Tracks Length and Exposure", "size": 24, "bold": true, "font": "Merriweather"},
    {"id": "ps2_tbl", "type": "table", "x": 0.5, "y": 1.0, "w": 9, "size": 14, "font": "Lato", "row_h": 0.43,
   "col_widths": [4.5, 1.5, 1.5, 1.5], "header": true, "col_align": ["START", "END", "END", "END"],
   "rows": [["Model (Pearson r with human means)", "Length", "Exposure", "Word types"],
            ["TRELLIS", ".93", ".90", "−.96"],
            ["TRELLIS, concepts off", ".77", ".63", "−.99"],
            ["Transitional probability (ours)", ".76", ".75", "−.94"],
            ["Transitional probability (Frank et al.)", ".84", ".43", "−.99"],
            ["Lexical model (Frank et al.)", ".94", ".89", "−.98"],
            ["PARSER (Frank et al.)", ".00", ".86", ".00"],
            ["TRACX (French et al., 2011)", ".92", "–", ".97"]]},
  {"id": "ps2_take", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 4.5, "w": 8.4, "h": 0.4, "text": "Removing the concepts flattens the gain with exposure.", "size": 14, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ps2_foot", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 5.0, "w": 8.4, "h": 0.4, "text": "Frank et al. (2010), human means read from Figure 1 (about ±1 point); 12 simulated participants per condition.", "size": 11, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"},
  {"id": "ps2_num", "type": "text", "x": 9, "y": 5.1, "w": 0.5, "h": 0.4, "text": "31", "size": 12, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"}]},
 {"id": "ps3_slide", "layout": "BLANK", "index": 30, "elements": [
  {"id": "ps3_title", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.3, "w": 9, "h": 0.6, "text": "Digit Span Stalls Under EPAM's Storage Rule", "size": 24, "bold": true, "font": "Merriweather"},
  {"id": "ps3_bul", "type": "text", "x": 6.1, "y": 1.0, "w": 3.4, "h": 3.4, "bullets": true, "space_below": 8, "size": 14, "font": "Lato",
   "text": "DD's span grew from 7–9 to 104 digits over 865 sessions (Richman et al., 1995).\nTRELLIS replaces EPAM IV's hand-built network and keeps its procedure.\nAt one new chunk per list, span reaches 16.6; random labels give 16.0.\nStoring a chunk per 8 seconds of presentation reaches 69.8."},
  {"id": "ps3_cap", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 4.35, "w": 8.4, "h": 0.7, "text": "Mean list length over five-session blocks, three held-out simulated subjects (band = range). DD's points come from the text of Richman et al.", "size": 14, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ps3_num", "type": "text", "x": 9, "y": 5.1, "w": 0.5, "h": 0.4, "text": "32", "size": 12, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"}]},
 {"id": "ps4_slide", "layout": "BLANK", "index": 31, "elements": [
  {"id": "ps4_title", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.3, "w": 9, "h": 0.6, "text": "Grammar Learning Shows Chunk Strength Only", "size": 24, "bold": true, "font": "Merriweather"},
  {"id": "ps4_op", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.95, "w": 9, "h": 0.4, "text": "Endorsement effects, in percentage points:", "size": 14, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ps4_tbl", "type": "table", "x": 0.5, "y": 1.4, "w": 9, "size": 14, "font": "Lato", "row_h": 0.4,
   "col_widths": [6.0, 1.5, 1.5], "header": true, "col_align": ["START", "END", "END"],
   "rows": [["Effect", "People", "TRELLIS"],
            ["Grammaticality (Knowlton & Squire, 1996)", "22.0", "1.4"],
            ["Chunk strength by grammaticality interaction", "24.7", "23.6"],
            ["Grammaticality change with training (Meulemans & Van der Linden, 1997)", "9.2", "−8.8"]]},
  {"id": "ps4_bul", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 3.45, "w": 9, "h": 1.6, "bullets": true, "space_below": 8, "size": 14, "font": "Lato",
   "text": "TRELLIS endorses a string when its familiarity is above its own median.\nRemoving the concepts changes nothing; transitional probabilities fit better (r = .80 against .64).\nChunk strength at the string edges alone gives 21.5, so the familiarity behaves like chunk strength."},
  {"id": "ps4_num", "type": "text", "x": 9, "y": 5.1, "w": 0.5, "h": 0.4, "text": "33", "size": 12, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"}]},
 {"id": "ad1_slide", "layout": "BLANK", "index": 7, "elements": [
  {"id": "ad1_title", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.3, "w": 9, "h": 0.6, "text": "Addenda to the Theory", "size": 24, "bold": true, "font": "Merriweather"},
  {"id": "ad1_op", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 0.95, "w": 9, "h": 0.4, "text": "Version 2 keeps every postulate of version 1 and adds six addenda:", "size": 14, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ad1_h1", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 1.45, "w": 4.3, "h": 0.48, "text": "For version 2", "size": 16, "bold": true, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ad1_b1", "type": "text", "x": 0.5, "y": 1.98, "w": 4.3, "h": 3.0, "bullets": true, "space_below": 8, "size": 14, "font": "Lato",
   "text": "Parsing considers every segmentation into chunks and weighs each chunk by its share of the analyses.\nGeneration writes one element at a time, considering every chunk that could surround the position.\nLearning stores every candidate chunk in both taxonomies, weighted by its posterior."},
  {"id": "ad1_h2", "type": "text", "x": 5.2, "y": 1.45, "w": 4.3, "h": 0.48, "text": "For the memory studies", "size": 16, "bold": true, "font": "Lato"},
  {"id": "ad1_b2", "type": "text", "x": 5.2, "y": 1.98, "w": 4.3, "h": 3.0, "bullets": true, "space_below": 8, "size": 14, "font": "Lato",
   "text": "Short-term memory holds a few elements (seven, or four), each of which may be a chunk.\nThe familiarity of an experience is the stored weight of the chunks that span it.\nRecall of a damaged chunk uses its concept and its surviving parts as cues."},
  {"id": "ad1_num", "type": "text", "x": 9, "y": 5.1, "w": 0.5, "h": 0.4, "text": "8", "size": 12, "font": "Lato", "color": "#6B7280"}]}
]}
